<p>Capacity and floor space are relative and vary by product size, pitch, and line speed. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Buffer Sizing</h2>
<h3>Calculating Buffer Capacity</h3>
<p>Buffer capacity must cover the longest expected downstream stop without forcing an upstream shutdown. Three data points determine the required capacity:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Upstream production rate:</strong> Products per minute entering the buffer during a downstream stop. The buffer must absorb this flow for the duration of the stop.</li>
<li><strong>Downstream stop duration:</strong> The longest typical stop, changeover, refill, jam clearance, maintenance. Use a high-percentile stop duration (for example, the 90th percentile), not the average, to ensure the buffer covers most stops. Data from OEE tracking systems provides this number.</li>
<li><strong>Product pitch:</strong> The center-to-center spacing of products on the storage conveyor. Product length plus the gap between products. Tighter pitch stores more products per linear foot but may cause contact damage on sensitive products.</li>
</ol>
<p class="formula"><strong>Storage Length (ft) = (Products/min x Stop Duration (min) x Product Pitch (ft))</strong></p>
<p>Example: 40 bottles/min, 10-minute palletizer changeover, 4-inch pitch (0.33 ft):<br>
40 x 10 x 0.33 = 132 ft of storage conveyor</p>
<p>A serpentine buffer with six parallel lanes in a 25-foot run provides 150 linear feet of storage, sufficient for this example with margin. A vertical accumulation spiral can provide similar path length on a fraction of that floor space by stacking tiers.</p>
<h3>Overfill and Underfill Handling</h3>
<p>The storage conveyor must handle two edge cases. <strong>Overfill:</strong> upstream produces faster than downstream consumes, and the buffer fills completely. The storage conveyor must signal the upstream line to slow down or stop before product jams at the buffer infeed. A "buffer full" sensor near the infeed triggers this signal. <strong>Underfill:</strong> the buffer drains completely during normal operation. The "buffer empty" signal to the downstream line prevents it from running dry and faulting.</p>
<p>Multi-level fill sensors (full, 75%, 50%, 25%, empty) allow the line PLC to adjust upstream and downstream speeds proportionally rather than using hard start/stop transitions. This graduated control smooths line operation and prevents the oscillating start-stop cycle that degrades OEE. See the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a> for line-level control strategies.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Storage Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Beverage line buffering:</strong> Filled and labeled bottles accumulate in serpentine or mass-flow buffers between the filler/labeler section and the case packer. High-speed beverage lines make storage conveyors essential for absorbing the case packer's intermittent stops for case blank replenishment and tape cartridge changes.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-palletizer storage:</strong> Sealed cases accumulate in spiral or serpentine buffers before the palletizer. The buffer absorbs pallet change cycles, stretch wrap cycles, and slip-sheet insertion. As a sizing example, a 10-minute buffer at 30 cases per minute holds 300 cases. Compare that with your measured pallet change and wrap times.</p>
<p><strong>Between primary and secondary packaging:</strong> Individual packages (bottles, pouches, cartons) accumulate after primary packaging (filling, sealing) and before secondary packaging (case packing, shrink wrapping). This allows independent changeovers on primary and secondary equipment without stopping the other section.</p>
<p><strong>Shift-break buffering:</strong> Manual inspection or packing stations stop during operator breaks. A storage buffer sized to the break length upstream of the manual station allows the automated upstream line to continue producing during the break. The buffer drains when the operator returns.</p>
<p><strong>Quality hold:</strong> Some pharmaceutical and high-value packaging lines use storage conveyors as quality hold zones. Product enters the buffer and waits for lab test results before releasing to final packaging. The buffer holds product in FIFO sequence until the quality release signal arrives. Unreleased product diverts to a <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> if test results fail.</p>
<h2 id="bunkers">Bulk Bunkers and Seasonal Product</h2>
<p>Storage conveyors hold packaged or single-file product for minutes. Loose bulk product coming out of seasonal storage, such as potatoes, carrots, or onions held in boxes or bulk stores after harvest, needs a different tool: a bunker or holding hopper that receives crop in large batches and feeds the packing or grading line at a steady rate.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>How it works:</strong> A box or bag tipper empties crop into a hopper with a belt floor. <a href="https://tongengineering.com/product/even-flow-hopper/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tong describes its Even Flow hopper</a> as lowering its conveyor at the feed-on end to form a holding hopper, then using a level sensor to feed crop out evenly to graders, optical sorters, or packing machines.</li>
<li><strong>Sequence:</strong> A belt-floor bunker discharges crop roughly in the order it was loaded, but loose product mixes. If lots must stay separate for traceability, run the bunker empty between lots.</li>
<li><strong>Where it fits:</strong> Bunkers sit at the start of the line. Downstream, the packed product uses the storage and accumulation conveyors on this page.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/storage-tong-even-flow-hopper.webp" width="1000" height="672" alt="Potatoes spread across the blue belt floor of a steel hopper in a vegetable packhouse, with grading equipment behind it." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Tong even flow hopper holding potatoes. The belt floor feeds crop out to the line downstream. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://tongengineering.com/product/even-flow-hopper/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tong Engineering</a></span></figcaption></figure>
